WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
Coding Exercise 3 uses the anti-join pattern.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.597
You need to return patients who do not have any lab results.

00:00:07.597 --> 00:00:10.892
Start by preserving all patients with a LEFT JOIN.

00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:13.482
Then attempt to match lab rows.

00:00:13.482 --> 00:00:18.917
For patients with no lab result, the lab-side columns become NULL.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:24.072
Now choose a right-side column that cannot be NULL for a real matched row.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:29.017
The lab test ID is a good choice because it is a primary identifier.

00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:31.712
Filter for that key being NULL.

00:00:31.712 --> 00:00:37.232
Read your query as a sentence: keep all patients, try to match lab results,

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:41.177
then keep only the patients for whom no lab result was found.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:45.427
This anti-join pattern is common in real data-quality work.

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:52.322
Find customers with no purchases, employees with no required training, students with no submission,

00:00:52.322 --> 00:00:57.207
products with no inventory record, or patients with no follow-up visit.

00:00:57.207 --> 00:01:00.613
Once you understand the pattern, you will use it often.
